WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.570
One of the things I'm always trying to
fight is this idea that technology

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.711
is a cold weird thing. Or that mass
produce items whether it's a blender a

00:00:04.711 --> 00:00:09.840
car or computer are simply dead things
within themselves. Like the humans once

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.590
they get their hands on the things love
the things. And their love of the things

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.701
make these things come alive! So there is
a benefit in collecting things, right?

00:00:17.701 --> 00:00:21.750
Because there's a part of you that comes
in there. It becomes part of the story of

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.761
your life. The story you tell. It becomes
the talisman. And here's the thing...

00:00:26.761 --> 00:00:31.800
when I was in my teens, I started calling computer bulletin board systems

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.411
and i love them. I racked up incredible amounts of bills.

00:00:37.411 --> 00:00:42.039
This is another yearbook photo where i am posing

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:45.510
with my BBS. So, yeah, that's right.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.298
And you're like, wow, Jason you also

00:00:47.298 --> 00:00:48.641
have some weird stuff in there.

00:00:48.641 --> 00:00:49.681
Cryptographic message... how to make chicken littles

00:00:49.681 --> 00:00:54.751
from a KFC that one of my friends worked at.

00:00:54.751 --> 00:00:57.405
Lysol. There's a whole thing with...

00:00:57.405 --> 00:01:00.134
there's a whole thing with shop vacs.

00:01:00.134 --> 00:01:00.871
And that's why that's there. The DustBuster.

00:01:00.871 --> 00:01:03.111
I made sure that I had a DustBuster

00:01:03.111 --> 00:01:05.191
in every photo of me in the yearbook.

00:01:05.191 --> 00:01:08.414
I don't know sometimes I think, like,

00:01:08.414 --> 00:01:09.721
they're just this blowing door and I come out...

00:01:09.721 --> 00:01:11.421
I go 'WHAT are you thinking?!'

00:01:11.421 --> 00:01:12.211
And then leave again. No advice, no nothing, just:
Stop it!

00:01:12.211 --> 00:01:17.641
But, you know, anyway DustBusters.

00:01:17.641 --> 00:01:19.639
Love them. But the thing was was

00:01:19.639 --> 00:01:22.261
when I call bulletin board systems and I'm

00:01:22.261 --> 00:01:24.691
assuming people know. A bulletin board
system is a bulletin board system is

00:01:24.691 --> 00:01:27.000
basically a computer with a modem with

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.491
a lonely person at it who then keeps code up

00:01:29.491 --> 00:01:31.733
so that when other lonely people call
them

00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:33.601
they leave messages about how angry they are

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:35.510
at the world because they're lonely and then

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:36.421
other people disagree with them...

00:01:36.421 --> 00:01:39.895
And nobody gives anybody hugs, and, um --

00:01:39.895 --> 00:01:42.691
anyway so the point is that these things were kind of

00:01:42.691 --> 00:01:47.971
a self-made world, right? You would pay enormous

00:01:47.971 --> 00:01:48.471
amounts of money at the time to put
a computer on a phone line.

00:01:48.471 --> 00:01:52.111
I mean this is all very expensive.

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:55.901
And then other people would call and

00:01:55.901 --> 00:01:57.277
use your computer and post to it,

00:01:57.281 --> 00:01:59.501
but the thing was that they were transient

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:01.521
they were unbelievably transient.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.021
They would go away after like 5 or 6 months.

00:02:04.021 --> 00:02:06.841
And some of them would stay around for
a year or two or disappear.

00:02:06.841 --> 00:02:09.881
Jason, who had previously learned that the world

00:02:09.881 --> 00:02:13.581
was messed up, would back up everything he found.

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:17.661
So if I found anything on a website that I liked. That

00:02:17.661 --> 00:02:22.381
told me about the place or had quote unquote 'text files'

00:02:22.381 --> 00:02:23.361
where you could go and find out

00:02:23.361 --> 00:02:25.461
how to make a bomb, how to fix the modem,

00:02:25.461 --> 00:02:28.921
how to get angry at the world, 10 terrible lightbulb jokes,

00:02:28.921 --> 00:02:30.741
I just grabbed them all.

00:02:30.741 --> 00:02:33.181
I just grabbed them all and put them all on floppy disks.

00:02:33.181 --> 00:02:34.901
Until I have a stack of floppy disks.

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:37.321
And when bulletin board systems stopped

00:02:37.321 --> 00:02:40.221
being important in my life--okay, that's not right.

00:02:40.221 --> 00:02:42.981
When bulletin board systems stopped
being a thing I did daily...

00:02:42.981 --> 00:02:46.661
I ended up going to you know college and

00:02:46.661 --> 00:02:48.461
I found a way to get on through the MIT system

00:02:48.461 --> 00:02:51.321
into the Internet at like 18 or 19,

00:02:51.321 --> 00:02:56.101
and, you know, once you're doing the heroin
it's hard to go back to marijuana

00:02:56.101 --> 00:03:01.461
And so I ended up storing away those floppies.

00:03:01.461 --> 00:03:07.481
And in 01998 I thought,
"Man, I sure had fun on those BBSes!"

00:03:07.481 --> 00:03:09.821
And now I'm 28. And the web is up.

00:03:09.821 --> 00:03:14.261
And the web has everything now. It's got Alta Vista.

00:03:14.261 --> 00:03:17.781
It's pretty. It's got lots of... the text will center now.

00:03:17.781 --> 00:03:21.421
You can effect the font. The sky's the limit!

00:03:21.421 --> 00:03:23.181
So let me go look up about my favorite

00:03:23.181 --> 00:03:26.321
Bulletin Board System: Sherwood Forest 9143591517

00:03:26.321 --> 00:03:28.821
And let's go find out what's going on with them.

00:03:28.821 --> 00:03:30.246
No information. That's weird.

00:03:30.246 --> 00:03:32.581
How about bulletin board systems of New York?

00:03:32.581 --> 00:03:35.021
Nothing. Ooh. How about bulletin board systems?

00:03:35.021 --> 00:03:38.301
Not much. All right. What happened here?

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:41.981
Now, friggin 20 years of research has
revealed to me what happened

00:03:41.981 --> 00:03:43.541
I'll tell you in a moment, it's not that interesting.

00:03:43.541 --> 00:03:46.041
But basically bulletin board systems

00:03:46.041 --> 00:03:48.061
were persona non grata on the Internet.

00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:52.581
And so I was like, but I have all these old text files!

00:03:52.581 --> 00:03:55.068
Well, why don't I put them all up?

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.801
So I ended up grabbing textfiles.com

00:03:57.801 --> 00:04:01.581
and I put up 50,000 text files
that i had collected over those years.

00:04:01.581 --> 00:04:04.141
And all these people came out of the woodwork to go

00:04:04.141 --> 00:04:07.121
first: oh my god all these old things!

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:11.080
And, more importantly: I have stuff and I don't want it.

00:04:11.081 --> 00:04:14.201
You obviously do. Would you like the stuff now?

00:04:14.201 --> 00:04:17.101
And I'm like: Yes! Yes, please send me the stuff!

00:04:17.101 --> 00:04:18.648
Send me cd-roms. Send me piles of floppies.

00:04:18.648 --> 00:04:19.691
Send me whatever you want to.

00:04:19.691 --> 00:04:25.141
And I'll pull them up on textfiles.com. and textfiles.com grew

00:04:25.151 --> 00:04:28.769
and grew and grew and I became known,

00:04:28.769 --> 00:04:31.961
whether I wanted to (or not) as a historian.

00:04:31.961 --> 00:04:34.241
As an archivist; as a librarian.

00:04:34.241 --> 00:04:37.141
I did not go to school for these things.

00:04:37.141 --> 00:04:40.381
I went to school with a film degree, okay?

00:04:40.381 --> 00:04:43.541
I graduated from high school, this is very true,

00:04:43.541 --> 00:04:45.001
this is not me being funny.

00:04:45.001 --> 00:04:48.441
I graduated from high school with
a 1.7 grade point average

00:04:48.451 --> 00:04:51.891
That is very hard to do without shooting the place up

00:04:51.891 --> 00:05:00.241
But I did it. I did it!

